SOL={
'q-2493':([17592186061923],r'Using $\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dy}\arcsin y=\frac1{\sqrt{1-y^2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\int\frac9{\sqrt{1-y^2}}\,\mathrm dy=9\arcsin y+C.\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on $-1<y<1$.'),
'q-2497':([17592186061929],r'Using $\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dx}\arctan x=\frac1{1+x^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\int\frac3{1+x^2}\,\mathrm dx=3\arctan x+C.\n$$'),
'q-31244':([17592186062441],r'The exponential function is its own derivative. Therefore\n\n$$\n\int3e^x\,\mathrm dx=3e^x+C.\n$$'),
'q-31248':([17592186062454],r'For $a>0$ and $a\ne1$, $\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dx}a^x=a^x\ln a$. Hence\n\n$$\n\int4\cdot5^x\,\mathrm dx=\frac{4\cdot5^x}{\ln5}+C.\n$$'),
'q-31311':([17592186062456],r'Integrate each term:\n\n$$\n\int(2^x-10e^x)\,\mathrm dx=\frac{2^x}{\ln2}-10e^x+C.\n$$'),
'q-49077':([17592186063143],r'The derivative of $\operatorname{arcsec}x$ is $\frac1{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}$ on $|x|>1$. Thus\n\n$$\n\int\left(\frac3{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}+12\right)\,\mathrm dx=3\operatorname{arcsec}x+12x+C.\n$$\n\nThe constant may be chosen independently on the intervals $(-\infty,-1)$ and $(1,\infty)$.'),
'q-49149':([17592186063173],r'Rewrite the numerator as $x^2-4=(x^2+1)-5$. Then\n\n$$\n\begin{aligned}\int\frac{x^2-4}{x^2+1}\,\mathrm dx&=\int\left(1-\frac5{x^2+1}\right)\,\mathrm dx\\&=x-5\arctan x+C.\end{aligned}\n$$'),
'q-51102':([17592186063395],r'Integrate term by term:\n\n$$\n\begin{aligned}\int(2e^x+4^x-2x)\,\mathrm dx&=2e^x+\frac{4^x}{\ln4}-x^2+C.\end{aligned}\n$$'),
'q-105893':([17592186075111],r'An inhibited decay equation is proportional to the difference between the limiting value and the current value. With limiting value $40$ and rate constant $20$, it is\n\n$$\n\frac{\mathrm dy}{\mathrm dt}=20(40-y)=800-20y.\n$$\n\nFor $y>40$ this derivative is negative and approaches zero as $y$ approaches $40$.'),
'q-64658':([17592186077735],r'Substitute $y=e^{\lambda x}$ into the equation and divide by the nonzero factor $e^{\lambda x}$:\n\n$$\n\lambda^2-5\lambda+6=(\lambda-2)(\lambda-3)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\lambda=2$ or $\lambda=3$.'),
'q-64659':([17592186077739],r'The characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\n\lambda^2-5\lambda-6=(\lambda-6)(\lambda+1).\n$$\n\nThus all solutions are linear combinations of $e^{6x}$ and $e^{-x}$. The option $e^{5x}$ is not such a solution: substitution gives\n\n$$\n(25-25-6)e^{5x}=-6e^{5x}\ne0.\n$$'),
'q-64704':([17592186077748],r'The exponential trial solution leads to\n\n$$\n\lambda^2+9\lambda+18=(\lambda+6)(\lambda+3)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\lambda=-6$ or $\lambda=-3$.'),
'q-7784':([17592186078022],r'The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\lambda^2-3\lambda-10=(\lambda-5)(\lambda+2)=0.\n$$\n\nThe two distinct roots are $5$ and $-2$, so the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{-2x}+Be^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary real constants.'),
'q-15219':([17592186084841],r'The decay model has solution $N(t)=230000e^{-kt}$. Use the measurement after five years:\n\n$$\n23000=230000e^{-5k}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad k=\frac{\ln10}{5}\approx0.460517.\n$$\n\nThe continuous decay rate is approximately $46\%$ per year, to the nearest percent.'),
'q-7600':([17592186086588],r'Compare with $\frac{x^2}{a^2}+\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1$. Here $a=\sqrt{10}$ and $b=3$, with $a>b$. The lengths of the major and minor axes are therefore\n\n$$\n2a=2\sqrt{10},\qquad2b=6.\n$$'),
'q-7605':([17592186086608],r'The graph crosses the $x$-axis at $\pm3$ and the $y$-axis at $\pm4$. Thus the horizontal and vertical semiaxes are $3$ and $4$, respectively. Its equation is\n\n$$\n\frac{x^2}{3^2}+\frac{y^2}{4^2}=1,\qquad\text{or}\qquad\frac{x^2}{9}+\frac{y^2}{16}=1.\n$$'),
'q-83143':([17592186087148],r'The initial depth is $D(0)=300$ feet. Solving $D^{\prime}=0.01D$ gives\n\n$$\nD(t)=300e^{0.01t}.\n$$\n\nAfter six years,\n\n$$\nD(6)=300e^{0.06}\approx318.550964\text{ feet}.\n$$\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, the depth is $319$ feet.'),
'q-83151':([17592186087153],r'The solution is $V(t)=4800e^{kt}$. Apply the value after three years:\n\n$$\n7200=4800e^{3k}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad k=\frac{\ln(3/2)}3\approx0.135155.\n$$\n\nThe continuous annual growth rate is $14\%$, to the nearest percent.'),
'q-8363':([17592186087184],r'The solution with the given initial price is\n\n$$\nP(t)=250000e^{0.0325t}.\n$$\n\nAfter $25$ years,\n\n$$\nP(25)=250000e^{0.8125}\approx563383.6968.\n$$\n\nRounded to the nearest dollar, the price is $\$563384$.'),
'q-8843':([17592186087319],r'Set $z=y^2$, so $z^{\prime}=2yy^{\prime}$. Multiplying the ODE by $2y$ gives\n\n$$\nz^{\prime}=x+\frac{3z}{x}.\n$$\n\nOn an interval with $x\ne0$, multiply $z^{\prime}-3z/x=x$ by $x^{-3}$:\n\n$$\n\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dx}(zx^{-3})=x^{-2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating gives $zx^{-3}=-x^{-1}+K$, hence\n\n$$\ny^2=Kx^3-x^2.\n$$\n\nThe original ODE requires $x\ne0$ and $y\ne0$. This implicit expression covers both sign branches on intervals where $Kx^3-x^2>0$.'),
'q-11816':([17592186089246],r'The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\frac{\mathrm df}{\mathrm dx}=e^{3x}\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dx}(3x)=3e^{3x}.\n$$'),
'q-48628':([17592186090607],r'Differentiate the outer exponential and multiply by the derivative of its exponent:\n\n$$\n\frac{\mathrm dy}{\mathrm dx}=e^{x^4+1}\cdot4x^3=4x^3e^{x^4+1}.\n$$'),
'q-48631':([17592186090612],r'For a constant base, $\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dx}a^{u(x)}=a^{u(x)}\ln(a)u^{\prime}(x)$. With $u(x)=6x^2-4$,\n\n$$\n\frac{\mathrm dy}{\mathrm dx}=3^{6x^2-4}\ln(3)\cdot12x.\n$$'),
'q-11414':([17592186093807],r'Let $u=3x^5-4x^2$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\begin{aligned}f^{\prime}(x)&=3u^2u^{\prime}\\&=3(3x^5-4x^2)^2(15x^4-8x).\end{aligned}\n$$'),
'q-119078':([17592186094096],r'On an interval avoiding $x=1$, rearrange and integrate:\n\n$$\ny^{\prime}=-\frac2{(x-1)^2}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad y=\frac2{x-1}+C.\n$$\n\nThe condition $y(2)=1$ gives $1=2+C$, so $C=-1$. Thus\n\n$$\ny=\frac2{x-1}-1,\qquad x>1.\n$$\n\nThe interval $(1,\infty)$ is the maximal interval containing the initial point.'),
'q-14245':([17592186094684],r'Rearrange as $f^{\prime}(x)=-11x$ and integrate:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\int-11x\,\mathrm dx=-\frac{11}{2}x^2+C.\n$$'),
'q-48625':([17592186095425],r'With $u=x^4+3x$, we have $f=u^2$ and $u^{\prime}=4x^3+3$. Therefore\n\n$$\nf^{\prime}(x)=2(x^4+3x)(4x^3+3).\n$$'),
'q-48846':([17592186095474],r'Write $y=(4x-3)^{1/2}$ and apply the chain rule:\n\n$$\ny^{\prime}=\frac12(4x-3)^{-1/2}\cdot4=\frac2{\sqrt{4x-3}}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative is defined for $x>3/4$.'),
'q-49942':([17592186095669],r'Rearrange and integrate:\n\n$$\nf^{\prime}(x)=-\frac12e^x\quad\Longrightarrow\quad f(x)=-\frac12e^x+C.\n$$\n\nUsing $f(0)=-1$ gives $-1=-1/2+C$, hence $C=-1/2$. Therefore\n\n$$\nf(x)=-\frac12(e^x+1).\n$$'),
'q-37376':([17592186123001],r'Inhibited growth is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. The capacity is $85$, so the model is\n\n$$\n\frac{\mathrm dR}{\mathrm dt}=k(85-R),\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nThe initial population $20$ determines the initial condition $R(0)=20$; it does not change the limiting capacity.'),
'q-333512':([17592186149842,17592186149846],r'The relation $k_1+k_2\sin x=0$ must hold for every real $x$. At $x=0$ it gives $k_1=0$. At $x=\pi/6$ it gives\n\n$$\nk_1+\frac12k_2=0.\n$$\n\nCombining these equations gives $k_2=0$. Thus only the trivial coefficients work, so $1$ and $\sin x$ are linearly independent.'),
'q-333518':([17592186149862,17592186149867,17592186149870],r'Differentiate $y_1=\cos(2x)$ and $y_2=\sin(2x)$, then compute\n\n$$\n\begin{aligned}W(y_1,y_2)&=y_1y_2^{\prime}-y_2y_1^{\prime}\\&=\cos(2x)\cdot2\cos(2x)-\sin(2x)\cdot(-2\sin(2x))\\&=2(\cos^2(2x)+\sin^2(2x))\\&=2.\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is nonzero for every real $x$, so the functions are linearly independent.'),
'q-64745':([17592186150177],r'Using $W(y_1,y_2)=y_1y_2^{\prime}-y_2y_1^{\prime}$, we get\n\n$$\nW=(x^2-1)\cdot1-x\cdot2x=-x^2-1.\n$$'),
'q-331177':([17592186164166],r'Follow the arrows from the marked point. The trajectory initially moves upward and to the right. It reaches the steeper dashed nullcline with a vertical tangent, turns left, and then crosses the other dashed nullcline with a horizontal tangent. Afterward it bends downward and left. The matching trajectory is\n\n![](images/67/67273ae9c0f9d50f1a95da3c61039fa9cf262a7b34b93cc9e6f1fe26cd463a5f.png)'),
'q-332632':([17592186164340,17592186164348],r'On the $x$-nullcline, $x^{\prime}=0$, so\n\n$$\n3x+y=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad y=-3x.\n$$\n\nOn the $y$-nullcline, $y^{\prime}=0$, so\n\n$$\n-x+2y=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad y=\frac12x.\n$$')}
